    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-237a058efc6a"
      "A fixed link refuses a fragment as unsupported-message whatever its form: a best-effort fragment, one with an extension, an empty one, and ones whose sequence number is noncanonical or above 32 bits are each exercised in fixed mode and each refused as unsupported-message, not as the class a heap link would give. It keeps every refusal and behaviour the stock-peer exam pins; the retained decoder, session, encoder and link tests and the six stock-peer exam tests (zenoh_stock::the_stock_peer_exam among them) run and pass by name beside the links tests, including the three session tests the stock-peer change added, and the stock-peer exam transcript is judged by the stock judge on this path, so deleting or renaming one of them fails the links gate and not only zenoh_stock_check. A large link delivers a 1024-byte payload whole and refuses 1025 bytes as over-bound although they would fit its batch, a demo link refuses 13 as over-bound, and a link declared with four keys and four subscribers holds them: a fifth subscriber is refused as subscriber-limit and a fifth alias as over-bound."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_links_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 286; } "The checker counts what it matched: 3 configurations, 56 scenarios, 15 stock fragments, 3 reassemblies, 3 fragments of our own Put, 2 edge lines, 2 session-sequence lines, 1 storage-reuse line, 1 declared-payload line, 1 notice-queue line, 1 aliased line, 12 deadline lines, 4 subscriber-id lines, 6 batch-edge lines, 2 fixed-edge lines, 2 minimum-configuration lines, 2 long-key alias lines, 2 single-fragment lines, 1 nine-slot line, 1 fixed nine-slot line, 6 wide-count lines, 2 slot lines, 9 peer-batch lines, 1 multibyte-key line, 1 underfilled-run line, 16 sequence-width boundary lines, 1 short-key line, 2 high-alias lines, 6 reuse-after-refusal lines, 15 width-step fragment lines, 9 declared-payload send lines, 1 drained-queue line and 3 stack-size lines, 192 positive cases, and 94 refusals, 286 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the fragment bytes and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-237bcc053439"
      "In heap mode the component supplies the storage: a link built over component-supplied storage reassembles a fragmented message into it, and the library takes no allocator of its own. A message whose slices the storage cannot hold is refused as out-of-memory and leaves the session open and unchanged, a message larger than the declared heap is refused as over-bound, a gap in the fragment sequence is refused as fragment-sequence, a run whose first fragment is not the session's next expected sequence number (replayed from before it, or skipping ahead of it) is refused as sequence-mismatch and leaves the session unchanged, and a whole accepted run advances that number by the number of fragments, wrapping at 32 bits, a reassembly that does not finish within the declared timeout is refused as reassembly-timeout and releases its storage, and a whole FRAME arriving while a message is being reassembled is refused as unexpected-message. The sender splits a Put of the declared 16384 bytes body for body as the host reference does (nine fragments at a 2048-byte batch, the payload length a three-byte field), and a Put of the declared 16384 bytes reassembles into the declared heap, and one byte more is refused as over-bound even when the storage would hold it. A fragment longer than the announced batch less the two-byte stream length is refused as length-exceeds-batch. A fragmented PUSH is held to what a whole-frame one is held to and nothing more: the extension flag on the PUSH, a timestamp or an encoding on the Put, an extension that is not the attachment and a chained one are refused with the classes the corpus gives them (unsupported-extension, unsupported-message, unsupported-message, unsupported-extension, unsupported-extension), and a zero-length key as invalid-keyexpr. A fragment whose sequence number ends before it is complete is refused as truncated on a heap link and as unsupported-message on a fixed one. A configuration of nine keys, nine subscribers and nine outstanding samples is built and filled to its limits through the link, not only judged by the contract: nine keys and nine subscribers are declared, the eighteen notices of nine samples are held undrained, each a distinct payload, and one more message is reassembled, with every payload read back; the tenth subscriber is refused as subscriber-limit and the tenth alias as over-bound, so a link whose tables or queue are sized for four fails. The same nine keys, nine subscribers and nine samples are built and filled in fixed mode, where each of the eighteen notices carries a copy of its payload inline and nothing is reassembled, with the tenth subscriber refused as subscriber-limit and the tenth alias as over-bound, so a fixed link that keeps four-entry arrays fails. The counts are also taken past the widths they could be stored in, in each mode: 256 keys, 256 subscribers and 256 samples (one past what a byte holds, 512 notices held undrained, each a distinct payload that includes its own index, with the 257th subscriber refused as subscriber-limit and the 257th alias as over-bound), and 65535 keys and 65535 subscribers with one sample (the most the contract admits and all a 16-bit field holds), so a link that keeps a count in a byte or rejects its 256th slot or sample fails, and so does one that narrows a slot index below 16 bits. What a link sends is bounded by the smaller of its own batch and the batch the peer announced in its INIT, because the peer accepts no more and the link's send storage holds no more: a heap link of 4096 sends fragments of at most 87, 510 and 2046 bytes to peers announcing 89, 512 and 2048, and a link of 2048 is not lifted past its own storage by a peer announcing 4096; a fixed link, which cannot fragment, sends the largest message that fits the smaller batch whole and refuses one byte more as over-bound, a class this exam chooses where the profile names none. Our handshake and declarations cannot be fragmented either, so they must fit the peer's batch, which is checked at 89. A sender that ignores the peer's batch_size and sizes everything from its own therefore fails. A configuration of 65536 samples, one past what 16 bits hold, is built in each mode with its 131072 notices held undrained, each a distinct four-byte payload that is its own index, so a count or an index narrowed to 16 bits repeats a payload. In the 65535-slot configurations every slot is used, not only the first 256: all 65535 subscribers and all 65535 aliases are declared, a Put is carried to the subscriber and through the alias at slot 256 and at the last slot, and all 65535 subscribers are undeclared, so a link that keeps its next or free index in a byte never reaches slot 256 and fails (a key-expression alias has no undeclaration in the profile, so the aliases are declared and used only). A valid multibyte key is carried: a four-character, nine-byte key is declared and undeclared and a fragmented Put is sent and reassembled for it, and the same key with an x, five characters and ten bytes, is refused as over-bound against a declared 9, so a link that counts characters or assumes ASCII fails. A Put of 1048576 bytes (1 MiB) is sent and reassembled whole at a 4096-byte batch, far past the 16384 of the heap configuration, so a link with a hidden 16 KiB ceiling fails. Puts of 2097152 bytes (2 MiB, whose payload length is a four-byte field) and 268435456 bytes (256 MiB, the first with a five-byte field) are sent and reassembled too, so a payload-length writer that stops at three or four bytes fails. A sender may flush early, because a fragment carries at most the batch less the stream length and not exactly that: a run whose interior fragments carry 1000, 1 and 1500 bytes of slice reassembles, so a receiver that insists every fragment marked more fills the batch fails. The edge between a message that travels whole and one that is fragmented is pinned on both sides of each step in the width of the sequence number, at 127 and 128, 16383 and 16384, 2097151 and 2097152, and 268435455 and 268435456, the four steps from a one-byte sequence number to a five-byte one, where it moves by one byte, so a link that sizes the frame from a cached width sends a whole batch one byte over the announced one. A fixed frame is built at the widest sequence number, 4294967295, because the frames that fill the batch exactly only do so at that width (a frame at sequence number 1000 is three bytes shorter), so a sender or a buffer that fails only there is caught. A complete Put in a single reliable FRAGMENT with the more flag clear reassembles, at sequence number 1000 and at 4294967295, and advances the expected sequence number by one, wrapping to 0, so a receiver that insists the first fragment says more follows fails. An admitted long key is also declared as a key-expression alias, in the form that names its key in full (the encoder is shown to write the bytes the stock peer wrote), and a sender-mapped fragmented Put is resolved through it: a 2028-byte key as alias 1 at a batch of 2048, and a 4076-byte key as alias 0x8000 at a batch of 4096, so an implementation that sizes the subscriber and full-key storage to keyBytes but keeps the old 129-byte alias buffer fails. The declaration and the Put share one reliable channel, so the declaration is at sequence number 4294967294 and the fragments start at the next, 4294967295, and run across the wrap to 0 and 1, as a session that accepted the declaration expects; a run that does not start there is refused as sequence-mismatch. The reassembly timeout is varied, not only the pinned 5000 ms: heap configurations with a 250 ms, a 1 ms, a 65536 ms (past 16 bits), a 4294967296 ms (past 32 bits) and a 9223372036854775807 ms timeout are accepted by the contract, the session's clock being a 64-bit count of milliseconds, and for each the run whose last fragment arrives exactly the timeout after the first reassembles and the run one millisecond later is refused as reassembly-timeout, so a receiver that hard-codes one value expires too early or too late at the others, and one that stores a timeout in 16 or 32 bits, saturates it or rejects it expires the exact run early. The smallest configuration the contract admits is run in each mode, a one-byte key and a one-byte payload in a batch of 89, with one key, one subscriber and one sample: it is built, the key is declared and undeclared, and the Put travels whole in one frame, so a link that keeps a lower limit of three key bytes or twelve payload bytes fails. The same bounds are run in fixed mode, where nothing is fragmented and the whole Put travels in one frame: at a batch of 89 a 10-byte key is declared and a Put of 31 payload bytes, the most the contract lets the smallest batch hold, is sent whole; at a batch of 4096 a key of 1000 bytes, far past the demo's 129, is declared and a Put of 3046 payload bytes, which with its frame, built at the widest five-byte sequence number, fills the batch, is sent whole. So a fixed link that keeps the old 512 to 2048 range fails. Every edge, heap, fixed and minimum, also reports the batch its INIT_ACK announces and the batch the INIT_SYN a connector sends announces, each decoded from the handshake, and the sha256 of both, so an acceptor that announces the configured batch but a connector that announces another, which a peer would size its fragments for, fails; because the longest INIT_ACK is 87 bytes whatever batch it announces, so an encoder that announces another batch than the one configured, which a peer would size its fragments for, fails. The admitted edges run through the link, not only the contract: at a batch of 89 the INIT_ACK of 87 bytes with a 16-byte ZID and a 64-byte cookie fills the batch exactly, a 70-byte key is declared and undeclared in frames of exactly 89 bytes, and a 300-byte Put is fragmented and reassembled; at a batch of 4096 a 4076-byte key is declared and undeclared in frames of exactly 4096 bytes and a Put of 16384 bytes is fragmented and reassembled; and at a batch of 2048 a key of 2028 bytes, far past the 129 of the demo configuration, is declared, used by a fragmented 5000-byte Put and undeclared. Each reports the handshake and declaration lengths, the number of fragments, the sha256 of their bodies and the sha256 of the payload, which the checker computes from the host reference after the contract has accepted the configuration. A valid key shorter than the declared maximum reassembles (a 3-byte key that is the subscriber's own, not only the 129-byte demo key), and aliases 0x8000 and 0xffff, which have bit 15 set, are declared and resolve a sender-mapped fragmented Put. Every invalid key form the whole-frame decoder refuses is refused after reassembly: a trailing slash, a question mark and a hash as invalid-keyexpr, a dollar as wildcard-keyexpr, and a message that ends inside the bytes its key length declares as truncated. A subscriber id is any 32-bit value, zero included: ids 0, 0x7fffffff, 0x80000000 and 0xffffffff are declared and undeclared with the encodings pinned, zero being a valid subscriber id where alias scope zero is reserved and refused, so an implementation that uses zero as an empty-slot sentinel fails, so a reader or a slot of 16 or 31 bits fails. A PUSH whose body is a delete is refused as unsupported-message and one that ends before its body as truncated, and a message that ends in the middle of the scope, key length, attachment length or payload length of the reconstructed Put is refused as truncated, not defaulted. A continuation must carry the next sequence number: one that repeats the number before it or goes back is refused as fragment-sequence, as a gap is, so chunks are never joined out of order. A full key is global: one carrying a nonzero scope, which is relative to an alias, is refused as invalid-keyexpr. The more flag, not the payload length, decides where a message ends: an interior fragment that says no more follows ends the message early and is refused as truncated, and a last fragment that still says more follows leaves the run open and is refused as reassembly-timeout once the declared timeout passes with nothing further, with no sample delivered. With the eight queued sample notices of the heap configuration held and not drained, each a distinct payload of the declared 16384 bytes, a ninth message of the same size reassembles and every payload read back afterwards is the one that was sent, so none is overwritten and a partition of small slots plus one large one fails; once those samples are consumed another message of 16384 bytes reassembles into the same storage, so a link that never returns the leases of consumed samples fails. Two messages in a row reassemble independently, and a timed-out reassembly releases its storage: a message of 5000 payload bytes reassembles into storage of exactly one such message (5171 bytes) after a run that timed out in the same storage, which a buffer left leased would not allow. A fragment carries a sequence number and no message id, so what interleaving leaves behind is what is refused: fragments of two messages joined in one run are refused as trailing-bytes, and a best-effort fragment arriving during a reassembly as unsupported-message."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_links_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 286; } "The checker counts what it matched: 3 configurations, 56 scenarios, 15 stock fragments, 3 reassemblies, 3 fragments of our own Put, 2 edge lines, 2 session-sequence lines, 1 storage-reuse line, 1 declared-payload line, 1 notice-queue line, 1 aliased line, 12 deadline lines, 4 subscriber-id lines, 6 batch-edge lines, 2 fixed-edge lines, 2 minimum-configuration lines, 2 long-key alias lines, 2 single-fragment lines, 1 nine-slot line, 1 fixed nine-slot line, 6 wide-count lines, 2 slot lines, 9 peer-batch lines, 1 multibyte-key line, 1 underfilled-run line, 16 sequence-width boundary lines, 1 short-key line, 2 high-alias lines, 6 reuse-after-refusal lines, 15 width-step fragment lines, 9 declared-payload send lines, 1 drained-queue line and 3 stack-size lines, 192 positive cases, and 94 refusals, 286 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the fragment bytes and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-237c5be17210"
      "A fragment that is not exactly the reliable form is refused with its class: a best-effort fragment as unsupported-message, one carrying extensions as unsupported-extension, one with no data as malformed-message, and one before the session is open as unexpected-message. A fragmented Put is held to what a single-batch Put is held to, in heap mode: without the attachment, with a 32-byte one, or with a GID length byte of 15 or 17 it is refused as malformed-message, with a 34-byte one as over-bound, with alias scope 0 as invalid-keyexpr, with an alias the session never declared (scope 2 with only alias 1 declared) as invalid-keyexpr, with a scope above 65535 as leb128-overflow, and with a 130-byte full key as over-bound (the 129-byte demo key reassembles). Every variable-length integer is read as the Rust reader reads it, on the link and not only in the host oracle: a fragment's sequence number, and the scope, the key length, the attachment length and the payload length of the reassembled Put, are each refused as leb128-noncanonical when overlong (a sequence number of 26 80 00 aa, a scope of 81 00) and as leb128-overflow when above their width (a sequence number above 32 bits, a key length above 16 bits, an attachment length or a payload length above 32 bits). An aliased Put from a peer that declared the alias is sender-mapped (header 0x5d) and reassembles, while the same Put receiver-mapped (0x1d), which names an alias this side declared and it declares none, is refused as invalid-keyexpr, and an alias that stands for a key other than the subscriber's is refused as undeclared-key. Values inside each declared width are read, not only values above it: a scope of 256, a key length of 256, an attachment length of 65536 and a payload length of 65536 are canonical in their fields and are refused for what they say (invalid-keyexpr for an alias the session never declared, over-bound for the three lengths), not as an overflow, so a reader that is too narrow fails. A payload shorter than its length says is refused as truncated. A reassembly is measured from its first fragment: it completes when the last arrives exactly the declared timeout (5000 ms) after it, and the same run one millisecond later is refused as reassembly-timeout, both pinned with a first timestamp of 7000000 ms, long after the clock passed the timeout, and a middle fragment one millisecond before the edge, so a deadline compared with the absolute clock and one refreshed by each continuation both fail, the rule the session's lease uses, so a timeout shorter than declared fails and so does a longer one. The top bit of each width is read too, so a reader one bit narrow fails: a scope of 32768 and a key length of 32768 (canonical in 16 bits), and an attachment length and a payload length of 2147483648 (canonical in 32), are refused for what they say (invalid-keyexpr for the undeclared alias, over-bound for the three lengths), not as overflows. A message that ends inside its 33-byte attachment is refused as truncated. A Put split across a sequence-number width change is pinned body for body against the host reference, one run from each step of the encoding (127, 16383, 2097151 and 268435455) and one across the 32-bit wrap (4294967294, whose headers shrink from five bytes to one), so a link that sizes every slice from its first fragment's header and overruns the announced batch fails. After each way a reassembly can end without delivering a sample, a sequence gap, a whole FRAME in the middle, a best-effort fragment in the middle, and a completed message refused for a malformed Put, a key the subscriber did not declare or a payload over the declared size, a message of 5000 payload bytes reassembles into storage of exactly one message, so a refusal that leaves the buffer leased fails. A full key is held to what a whole-frame key is held to: one with a wildcard is refused as wildcard-keyexpr, one with a leading or doubled slash or that is not UTF-8 as invalid-keyexpr, and a well-formed key other than the subscriber's as undeclared-key, so a reassembled Put cannot reach a topic the subscriber did not declare. The classes are the checker's, not the implementation's, and a fixed link refuses every fragment as unsupported-message whatever its form."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_links_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 286; } "The checker counts what it matched: 3 configurations, 56 scenarios, 15 stock fragments, 3 reassemblies, 3 fragments of our own Put, 2 edge lines, 2 session-sequence lines, 1 storage-reuse line, 1 declared-payload line, 1 notice-queue line, 1 aliased line, 12 deadline lines, 4 subscriber-id lines, 6 batch-edge lines, 2 fixed-edge lines, 2 minimum-configuration lines, 2 long-key alias lines, 2 single-fragment lines, 1 nine-slot line, 1 fixed nine-slot line, 6 wide-count lines, 2 slot lines, 9 peer-batch lines, 1 multibyte-key line, 1 underfilled-run line, 16 sequence-width boundary lines, 1 short-key line, 2 high-alias lines, 6 reuse-after-refusal lines, 15 width-step fragment lines, 9 declared-payload send lines, 1 drained-queue line and 3 stack-size lines, 192 positive cases, and 94 refusals, 286 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the fragment bytes and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28"; }; };
