  }) with {
    nonGoals = {
      "Judging what the idle ros2-demo-subscriber prints: it is in the composition and holds no network authority, and its output is neither required nor judged here. The exam reads the connector's lines only.";
      "Any peer but one eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 wheel on x86_64 Linux, any other port, a router or client peer, rmw_zenoh interoperability, liveliness tokens, queryables, and fragmented messages.";
      "Inbound connections to the guest, a guest listener on a NIC, DNS, scouting of any kind, and a hostname destination.";
      "A physical NIC. QEMU user networking is the only link observed; its translation of the guest's connection to the host's loopback is not the wire a physical network would show.";
      "Running a stock peer in CI. The wheel comes from a registry, so the recipe is an explicit local experiment in no aggregate.";
      "Any product change in this item's planning PR: no system spec, no generated composition, no guest change, and no modified schema. They are the implementation's.";
      "rmw_zenoh peer interoperability, liveliness tokens, queryables, routers, gossip, multicast scouting and transport security: R1 and later own them.";
      "A physical Raspberry Pi 5 claim. Every observation here is a host or AArch64 QEMU observation.";
      "Editing R0 or any other pre-cutoff item. R0 is closed by hand on recorded evidence once this epic's acceptance holds.";
      "The ROSIDL importer and RIHS01 type-hash generator: the demo contract's checker derives those values today and this epic consumes them as frozen constants.";
      "Session state, timers, sequence-number management and the TCP link: the session slice owns them.";
      "Encoding any message the decoder refuses.";
      "Opening a socket, waiting on a Notification or reading a clock capability: the transport runtime slice owns them.";
      "Reconnection or resumption across a link loss; a new link is a new session under a fresh identity.";
      "A general ROSIDL importer, strings, sequences, nested types, CDR2 or big-endian output: the admitted message has two fixed-width fields.";
      "The RIHS01 type-hash and key-expression derivation, which the demo contract's checker owns today.";
      "Qualifying the network service's loopback stack, which has its own gates.";
      "Any ROS node API, executor or logging: the node slice owns them.";
      "An async runtime or a general heap: the profile's blocking, fixed-buffer shape is chosen to need neither.";
      "A general ROS 2 node API, executor, parameters or graph introspection; the nodes provide the demo's publish and receive behaviour and its log lines.";
      "Any grant to a router, gossip, multicast, discovery, DNS or a filesystem.";
      "Observing the loopback stack's own wire. The exchange crosses the service's loopback backend, so there is no packet capture; this observes the node codecs and session at the service's application boundary.";
      "Interoperability with an external rmw_zenoh peer, which R1 owns.";
      "Running the harness in CI. It needs the upstream crates from a registry, so the recipe is explicit and is not part of any aggregate.";
      "rmw_zenoh interoperability, a live Zenoh peer, or any packet capture. The harness compares codec and session behaviour in process.";
      "Changing the Profile 0 codec or session. A disagreement the harness finds is fixed by a separate change.";
      "Adding the zenoh crates to the root Cargo.lock, the closures, or the product build.";
      "rmw_zenoh interoperability, liveliness tokens, queryables, requests, a router or client peer, scouting or gossip, wildcard key expressions, best-effort frames, fragmentation and transport security. The 1.0.0 Python binding has no liveliness API, so no liveliness token was ever captured and none is claimed.";
      "Running a stock peer in CI. The capture is replayed from bytes; the live peer needs the eclipse-zenoh wheel from a registry and is an explicit, local experiment.";
      "A stock peer in the guest, a NIC, an external listener or a host-reachable port. This item is the Profile 0 session and link; reaching it from outside QEMU is a later item.";
      "Changing the existing Profile 0 refusals the first release still owns: 101 of the 102 refused vectors in vectors.txt keep their name, class and bytes, and the other one, transport_keep_alive, the single byte 0x04 that the stock peer sent four times, the checker names and lets be removed or stay refused. It may not become an accepted row: every accepted row is read by consumers that cannot be taught the stock syntax in the implementation PR (the encoder gate rebuilds each from its summary, the cross-check harness encodes each, and a Rust test counts them). No other refused vector may become accepted: the existing vectors with related names (an extension flag, an alias, a scope, a suffix, an OAM) were compared byte for byte with the capture and none matches, because the stock peer attaches a QoS extension and a body they lack. No accepted row may be added for a stock batch; the stock tests exercise them. Positions no captured stock batch used, such as an extension on a frame header, a subscriber declaration or a Put header, or an undeclare, stay refused.";
      "Peer discovery or scouting: peers stay statically wired, and the guest's authority still refuses a scouting request. A router or client peer, rmw_zenoh interoperability, liveliness tokens, queryables and requests.";
      "The optional attachment: a Put without the 33-byte rmw_zenoh attachment is still refused, in every configuration.";
      "Unreliable (best-effort) fragments, extensions on a fragment, interleaved fragmented messages, and a message above the heap a configuration declares: each is refused by name, and none is made to work.";
      "Running a stock peer in CI. The fragments are replayed from bytes; the live peer needs the eclipse-zenoh wheel from a registry and is an explicit local experiment.";
      "Raising the demo's bounds: the demo configuration, its twelve-byte payload and its one key and one subscriber are unchanged, and the 100 refused vectors the stock-peer exam pins keep their name, class and bytes. A heap in components/lib itself: the library takes storage from the component and gains no alloc dependency.";
      "A guest-to-host or host-to-guest exchange. Booting a composition with a NIC is a later item; this one is judged on the host.";
      "The bare form without the QoS extension. A stock peer's encoder would send it when it carries no QoS, but the pinned vector usub_missing_wire_expr_ext refuses it and the stock-peer item holds that vector; the capture this item rests on has the QoS. Profile 0 keeps refusing it, and that is a declared limit.";
      "Matching an undeclaration to a key. The form carries none; the session matches by id alone, as the upstream router does.";
      "An undeclaration of a subscriber the peer declared through an alias in a later frame, a second subscriber, and any peer but one eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0.";
      "Any change to the external-listener item's composition, guest or listener. That item is replaced by a successor that admits the bare form in its capture judge.";
      "Any product change in this item's planning PR: no decoder, session or Rust test module. They are the implementation's.";
      "Declaring more than one subscription through a link configuration. That needs a new version of `zenoh-link-limits/v1` and re-admits the links item.";
      "Telling the owner which subscription matched a sample. The service core item adds that.";
      "Wildcard keys, and subscriptions to a key that the peer has not declared.";
      "Any change to what the peer may declare. `per_side` keeps its meaning.";
      "Any product change in the planning PR. The session and the Rust test module belong to the implementation.";
    };
  };

